sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/approach/murrays-law-vascular-optimality--claude-opus-5-5--3> a prov:Plan, delphi:TestableHypothesis ;
    rdfs:label "Synthetic-tree calibration: can image-derived radii distinguish exponent 3 from 7/3?" ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/murrays-law-vascular-optimality> ;
    dct:description "Exponent estimates from images are biased: estimating x from r₀^x = r₁^x + r₂^x is nonlinear, and radius noise near junctions inflates or deflates x. Before trusting approach 1, generate synthetic vascular trees with known exponent x_true ∈ {2, 7/3, 2.7, 3}, using constrained constructive optimization or a simple binary tree with stated asymmetry and length ratios. Render them through realistic image formation for each modality: a fundus model (2D projection, Gaussian blur, vessel central reflex, noise at matched SNR); light-sheet volumes for VesSAP (anisotropic PSF, voxel size); micro-CT. Run the same segmentation and radius pipeline as approach 1. Output: the bias and variance of estimated x as a function of x_true, vessel size in pixels and modality. These become the noise priors in approach 1's model, and determine the minimum vessel diameter (in pixels) at which 3 and 7/3 are distinguishable." ;

    delphi:proposedTest "Calibration curves giving, for each modality, the smallest vessel diameter at which estimated x separates 3 from 7/3 with 95% power. If no real dataset reaches that resolution, approach 1 is underpowered by design and the question needs better imaging." ;
    delphi:context "Fails if: Forward models fail validation: synthetic images differ visibly from real ones in vessel-contrast statistics, so the calibration does not transfer." ;
    delphi:hypothesisType "approach/simulation" ;
    skos:note "Steps: 1. Implement the synthetic tree generator and modality-specific forward image models. 2. Run approach 1's segmentation pipeline on 10^3 synthetic trees per setting. 3. Fit bias and variance curves for x as functions of x_true and diameter in pixels. 4. Publish calibration tables; set approach 1's minimum-diameter inclusion threshold from them." ;
    skos:note "Difficulty (proposer's estimate of completion within about a month of agent time): tractable" ;
    skos:note "Tool: simulation" ;
    skos:note "Tool: image-segmentation" ;
    skos:note "Tool: vascular-morphometry" ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch15-13> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch16-03> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://drive.grand-challenge.org/> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cell.2020.01.028> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1085/jgp.78.4.431> ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://github.com/fractastical/delphi/blob/ee98025a1960b300fcc06aa2106f51118b60f565/problems/approaches/claude-opus-5-5.json> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/murrays-law-vascular-optimality> a delphi:Concept, schema:Question ;
    rdfs:label "Does real vasculature obey Murray's law?" .

  <https://drive.grand-challenge.org/> dct:title "DRIVE" ;
    dct:description "Real images to validate the fundus forward model" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cell.2020.01.028> dct:title "Mapping the Fine-Scale Organization and Plasticity of the Brain Vasculature" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Kirst et al. 2020, Mapping the fine-scale organization and plasticity of the brain vasculature (imaging resolution for the forward model)" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1085/jgp.78.4.431> dct:title "On connecting large vessels to small. The meaning of Murray's law." ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Sherman 1981" .
}

